WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.600
This is almost surreal to say,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.900
but the channel recently
passed 1 million subscribers!

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.000
And I know what
you're thinking:

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.100
"just 48,576 more to go
before the next big milestone!"

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.900
And indeed, I'll hold off proper
celebrations until then.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.200
But, you know, seeing that seventh digit really
does make you reflect on what led to this point.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.780
And if I'm being honest,

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.920
a lot of the growth for this
channel just had to do

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.300
with some very kind people
sharing and promoting the content,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.340
both in terms of certain specific
shoutouts from creators with big audiences,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.320
and in terms of individuals
just sharing with their friends,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.460
so what I want to do now is take a moment to let
you all know about a few other math creators online

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.100
who I think you
would like a lot.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.960
There's just so much good creation that
happens under a lot of people's radars.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.600
First up, we have the channel "Think Twice".

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.340
I honestly have no idea why this channel isn't
better known among online math communities.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.460
It contains these really
beautifully done math animations,

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.480
and if you like this channel,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.400
you're definitely gonna like it.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.100
You absolutely get the sense
looking at any one of these

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.040
that it comes from someone who
thinks very visually about math.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.080
Naturally, this often takes the
form in some very pretty geometry

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.600
or some algebraic results that
are explained geometrically,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.380
and the focus tends to be a little bit more
short form and bite-sized pieces,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.160
so if you ever want a quick little
reminder of why math is beautiful,

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.560
This is just a great
channel to pop over to.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.440
Here, let me just let a couple more
of these animations just play out.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.840
Next up, I'd recommend you check out
LeiosOS, run by James Schloss.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.100
what I like about James is that he seems to think
first and foremost about community,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.800
and creating content seems to be more of
a vehicle for bringing people together online.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.580
Let me turn things over to him to explain
what it's all about, both on and off YouTube.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.260
James Schloss: LeiosOS is fundamentally a channel about
algorithms and works with the Arcane Algorithm Archive,

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.180
an open source and collaborative effort to document every
algorithm in existence in every language possible.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.760
We're just getting started and it's
obviously an impossible goal,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.380
but that's half the fun.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.900
This means that we cover a range of different
topics and here are some clips of the videos.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:34.060
Any light that enters a lens leaves on the other side
of the lens with the same angle it came in with.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.540
it's almost as if the light
never entered the lens at all,

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.880
and hence the name,
the invisible lens.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.480
If we take any arbitrary matrix, say, the identity
matrix with zeros along the 𝑤-dimension,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.740
the rotation doesn't
look quite right.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.880
This is because most tesseract
depictions use stereographic projections,

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.120
which resemble the act of holding
a light source behind an object

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.460
and checking a shadow
against the screen.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:04.560
Here, we divide our points into smaller packages and
use the Graham Scan to find their convex holes.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.840
We then take the vertices from the
holes and plug them into the Jarvis March

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.200
to create a wrapped 
gift of wrapped gifts.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.680
This is, of course, just a small subset
of the types of videos on our channel.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.900
Quickly, I would like to thank Grant for
making such amazing videos on 3Blue1Brown.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.560
It's inspiring to see such a
large and dedicated community

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.680
attempting to understand
the true beauty of mathematics.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.660
You guys are honestly amazing,

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.240
so even if I don't see you guys again,

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.540
please keep being such
an awesome community.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.360
Grant Sanderson: For my part, I think it would be
awesome to see this algorithm's archive grow more.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.240
both of the last two, at least on
YouTube, have shorter form content,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.140
So, next, let's switch to a channel
with more of a long-form focus:

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.600
Welch Labs, run by Stephen Welch.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.820
Now, I suspect many of you already
know about Stephen's work, for example,

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.380
the excellent series
"Imaginary Numbers are Real".

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.660
But there are many other great playlists from the
channel that you should absolutely take a look at.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.520
In fact, one of the things I like
most about this channel is that

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.620
Stephen thinks in terms of series
rather than in terms of individual videos.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.280
And each one of them includes clear
points where the viewer can engage

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.500
with the materials more than just
through a passive viewing experience,

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.360
often including workbooks
and associated PDFs,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.880
so the focus really is on learning
more so than just entertainment.

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:21.780
To get a feel here,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.760
let me just play some snippets from the start and then
from the end of his "Imaginary Numbers are Real" series,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.720
which should give you a pretty good
feel for the style in the scope,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.120
and I'll also throw in a snippet
from his series on machine learning.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.000
Stephen Welch: Algebraically, this new
dimension has everything to do

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.960
with a problem that was considered
impossible for over 2,000 years:

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.220
The square root of negative one.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.100
When we include this missing
dimension in our analysis,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.060
our parabola gets
WAY more interesting.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.420
Now that our input numbers are
in their full two dimensional form,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.340
We can see how our
function x²+1 really behaves.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.920
Our function does
cross the x-axis,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.620
We were just looking
in the wrong dimension.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:04.940
So why is this extra dimension that
numbers possessed not common knowledge?

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.780
Part of the reason is that it has
been given a terrible, terrible name.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.160
A name that suggests that
these numbers aren't even real!

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.620
All right, ready?

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.260
We'll draw the same
exact paths on 𝑤

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.560
and see how they show up on our Riemann
surface as they are mapped to our 𝑧-plane.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:38.780
[upbeat techno music]

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:44.080
So, why does our green path start out at one
location on our 𝑧-plane only to end up in another?

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.920
Simply because our green path leads
us to the other layer of our surface.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:54.200
From the perspective of our 𝑤-plane, it appears that
we've returned exactly to our starting point.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.660
But actually, we haven't.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.280
The 𝑤-plane is just a projection; a shadow.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.380
In reality, our path has led us to a
completely different branch of our function.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.240
[typing]

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.500
This is a decision tree.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.760
Right now, it's learning.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:17.980
When it's done, it'll have learned
to do something very human,

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:23.240
something that everyone knows how to do,
but no one can quite explain how they do it,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.080
something that is fooled
generations of brilliant scientists

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.820
with its apparent simplicity,
but practical complexity.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.920
something that has only become
possible in the last few decades

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.640
thanks to creative solutions
in the face of huge complexity:

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.380
it's learning to see.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.380
Let's test it out.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.600
With the help of a
camera and a computer,

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.560
our decision tree sees exactly
how many fingers were holding up.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.260
Now, depending on
who you are,

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:54.120
the fact that a machine can perform this
task may be completely mind-blowing,

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.820
not all that out
of the ordinary,

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.140
or may not even be
all that impressive.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.100
The good news here is
whichever camp you're in,

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.660
you're in good company.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:07.900
Our finger counting machine is a simple example that
belongs to a deceptively complex class of problems,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.820
problems that arise from
thinking about how we think.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.060
Grand Sanderson: And finally, turning
away from the YouTube world,

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.840
I want to highlight the blog
"Infinity Plus One".

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.760
The tone of the writing and the associated
visuals are both delightfully playful,

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:26.500
but the author James Dilts manages to get in some
pretty substantive math all while keeping it accessible.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:32.220
A particularly good sequence of posts is the
one he did on relativity, both special and general,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.570
and it's a little hard to directly feature
text posts and video forms,

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.540
but I'll leave you some links in the
description to some of my favorite posts,

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.920
and, of course, the description also has links for
the other three creators mentioned here.

